{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Extend "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Math"},{"type":"text","text":" with your own physical dimensions and units—currency, points, gameplay metrics, or anything else your app measures."}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CustomDimensions","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math"]]},"metadata":{"title":"Custom Dimensions and Units","roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"Math"}]},"kind":"article","primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Math"},{"type":"text","text":" ships with a comprehensive set of base dimensions ("},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/length","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", and friends) and derived dimensions ("},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/speed","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/energy","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", and more). These cover physics and everyday measurement, but your app almost certainly measures things that aren’t in the SI system too: money, hit points, ingredients, resource points, users, and so on."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The good news: ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension"},{"text":" is an open system. Because a dimension is ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"just a dictionary of exponent powers","type":"text"}]},{"text":", you can describe any dimension you can imagine at runtime.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Define a Custom Dimension","level":3,"anchor":"Define-a-Custom-Dimension"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Create a ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension"},{"text":" with a dictionary of exponent identifiers. Each key is a dimension name and each value is its power:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["import Math","","\/\/ A dimension for money, with a single base exponent.","let usdDimension = PhysicalDimension(exponents: [\"USD\": 1])","","\/\/ A dimension for \"users\", used later in per-user metrics.","let userDimension = PhysicalDimension(exponents: [\"user\": 1])"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You can build compound custom dimensions with the same arithmetic used","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"internally: ","type":"text"},{"code":"+","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" multiplies dimensions, ","type":"text"},{"code":"-","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" divides them, and ","type":"text"},{"code":"*","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" raises a dimension to a power:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let revenuePerUser = usdDimension - userDimension   \/\/ [\"USD\": 1, \"user\": -1]"]},{"type":"heading","text":"Define Custom Units","level":3,"anchor":"Define-Custom-Units"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Custom dimensions don’t have a compile-time type, so use","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/MathDimension\/unknown"},{"text":" as the type-level parameter and ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/NamedUnit"},{"text":" to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"create units. Pass a ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/LinearConverter"},{"text":" (for a simple scale) or an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/OffsetConverter"},{"text":" (for a scale plus an offset) to handle conversions:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let usdUnit = NamedUnit<MathDimension.unknown>(","    symbol: \"$\",","    dimension: usdDimension,","    converter: LinearConverter(coefficient: 1.0)",")","","\/\/ EUR is defined relative to the same base dimension.","let eurUnit = NamedUnit<MathDimension.unknown>(","    symbol: \"€\",","    dimension: usdDimension,","    converter: LinearConverter(coefficient: 1.09)",")"]},{"type":"aside","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Keep the same ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PhysicalDimension"},{"text":" value (here ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"usdDimension"},{"text":") for every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"unit in a family. The framework only converts between units whose dimensions","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"match, so sharing the exact same value makes your money interchangeable.","type":"text"}]}],"style":"tip","name":"Tip"},{"type":"heading","text":"Convert Between Custom Units","level":3,"anchor":"Convert-Between-Custom-Units"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Custom units convert exactly like the built-in ones:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let walletInEur = Quantity(value: 100.0, unit: eurUnit)","let walletInUsd = walletInEur.converted(to: usdUnit)","","print(walletInUsd.value) \/\/ 109.0"]},{"type":"heading","text":"Combine Custom Units with Built-In Ones","level":3,"anchor":"Combine-Custom-Units-with-Built-In-Ones"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Custom dimensions participate in dimensional arithmetic too. Here a currency","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"dimension combines with the built-in ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time"},{"type":"text","text":" dimension:"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let hourlyRate = Quantity(value: 50.0, unit: usdUnit) \/ Quantity(value: 1.0, unit: Units.hour)","","print(hourlyRate.value)          \/\/ 50.0","print(hourlyRate.unit.dimension) \/\/ [\"USD\": 1, \"time\": -1]","print(hourlyRate.unit.symbol)    \/\/ \"($\/h)\""]},{"type":"heading","text":"Use the Currency Dimension","level":3,"anchor":"Use-the-Currency-Dimension"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"If your custom dimension happens to be currency, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Math"},{"text":" already defines a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/currency","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" dimension and a built-in library of world","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"currencies (and cryptocurrencies) in ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/Units","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":". Currency units automatically","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"place their symbol before the value when formatting:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["let gift = Quantity(value: 100.0, unit: Units.usd)","print(gift.formatted()) \/\/ \"$100.00\"","","\/\/ Resolve units from ISO 4217 codes at runtime.","if let currency = Units.currency(for: \"SEK\") {","    print(currency.symbol) \/\/ \"kr\"","}"]},{"type":"heading","text":"What’s Next?","level":3,"anchor":"Whats-Next"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Learn how to lock custom units into a compile-time dimension in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Explore "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/Units","isActive":true},{"text":" to see the breadth of units provided out of the box.","type":"text"}]}]}]}],"kind":"content"}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Extending the Framework","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Extending-the-Framework"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/math\/customdimensions"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"references":{"doc://Math/documentation/Math/PhysicalDimension/time":{"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","title":"time","abstract":[{"text":"The dimension of time (SI base unit: second).","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time","url":"\/documentation\/math\/physicaldimension\/time","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"static"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"time","kind":"identifier"},{"kind":"text","text":": `Self`"}]},"doc://Math/documentation/Math/MathDimension/unknown":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"unknown","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The compile-time dimension type used for dimensions that don’t have a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"static type, such as custom dimensions created at runtime."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/MathDimension\/unknown","url":"\/documentation\/math\/mathdimension\/unknown","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","title":"MathDimension.unknown","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"unknown","